* 2.2.1::            Representing Sequences
* 2.2.2::            Hierarchical Structures
* 2.2.3::            Sequences as Conventional Interfaces
* 2.2.4::            Example: A Picture Language
@end menu

@node	2.2.1, 2.2.2, 2.2, 2.2
@subsection Representing Sequences

One of the useful structures we can build with pairs is a
@newterm{sequence}---an ordered collection of data objects.  There are, of
course, many ways to represent sequences in terms of pairs.  One particularly
straightforward representation is illustrated in @ref{Figure 2.4}, where the
sequence 1, 2, 3, 4 is represented as a chain of pairs.  The first element of
each pair is the corresponding item in the chain, and the second element of the
pair is the next pair in the chain.  The second element of the final pair
signals the end of the sequence by pointing to a distinguished value that is
not a pair, represented in box-and-pointer diagrams as a diagonal line and in
programs as the runtime's end-of-chain value @code{VNil}.  The entire sequence
is constructed by nested pair constructions:

@example kotlin
cons(VInt(1), cons(VInt(2), cons(VInt(3), cons(VInt(4), VNil))))
@end example

@float
@anchor{Figure 2.4}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 2.4:} The sequence 1, 2, 3, 4 represented as a chain of pairs.

@example
     +---+---+     +---+---+     +---+---+     +---+---+
---->| * | *-+---->| * | *-+---->| * | *-+---->| * | / |
     +-|-+---+     +-|-+---+     +-|-+---+     +-|-+---+
       |             |             |             |
       V             V             V             V
     +---+         +---+         +---+         +---+
     | 1 |         | 2 |         | 3 |         | 4 |
     +---+         +---+         +---+         +---+
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap2/Fig2.4e,,125mm,Figure 2.4: The sequence 1@comma{} 2@comma{} 3@comma{} 4 represented as a chain of pairs.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap2/Fig2.4e.std,,125mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 2.4:} The sequence 1, 2, 3, 4 represented as a chain of pairs.}
@end float

@noindent
Such a sequence of pairs, formed by nested pair constructions, is called a
@newterm{list}, and this edition's runtime provides a helper called
@code{vlist} to help in constructing lists.@footnote{In this book, we use
@newterm{list} to mean a chain of pairs terminated by the end-of-list marker.
In contrast, the term @newterm{list structure} refers to any data structure
made out of pairs, not just to lists.}  The above sequence could be produced by
@code{vlist(VInt(1), VInt(2), VInt(3), VInt(4))}.
In general,

@example kotlin
vlist(VInt(a1), VInt(a2), ..., VInt(an))
@end example

@noindent
is equivalent to

@example kotlin
cons(VInt(a1), cons(VInt(a2), ... cons(VInt(an), VNil) ...))
@end example

@noindent
The runtime's @code{toString} for chains prints lists the way Lisp systems
conventionally print them, as the sequence of elements enclosed in
parentheses.  Thus, the data object in @ref{Figure 2.4} is printed
as @code{(1 2 3 4)}:

@example kotlin
val oneThroughFour = vlist(VInt(1), VInt(2), VInt(3), VInt(4))

oneThroughFour
// => (1 2 3 4)
@end example

@noindent
Be careful not to confuse the expression
@code{vlist(VInt(1), VInt(2), VInt(3), VInt(4))} with the list
@code{(1 2 3 4)}, which is the string printed when the expression's value is
displayed.  The printed form is not itself a Kotlin expression; it is a
picture of the chain the expression builds.

We can think of the first element of a pair as selecting the head of the
list, and of the second element as selecting the sublist consisting of all but
the first item.  Nested selections can be used to extract the second, third,
and subsequent items in the list.@footnote{Since nested selections are
cumbersome to write, Lisp dialects provide abbreviations for them---for
instance, the @code{cadr} of a list is the @code{car} of its @code{cdr}.  The
names of all such procedures start with @code{c} and end with @code{r}, and
each @code{a} or @code{d} between them stands for one selection, applied in
the order in which it appears in the name.  Kotlin code simply nests the two
calls: the second item of a chain is @code{carOf(cdrOf(items))}.} The pair
construction makes a list like the original one, but with an additional item at
the beginning.  The runtime stores its pairs as the dynamic value @code{VPair},
so selecting from a chain needs a cast, which this edition packages as two
small helpers:

@example kotlin
fun carOf(v: Value): Value = (v as VPair).car

fun cdrOf(v: Value): Value = (v as VPair).cdr

fun numOf(v: Value): Long = (v as VInt).n
@end example

@noindent
With these, the list operations read as the book's own:

@example kotlin
carOf(oneThroughFour)
// => 1

cdrOf(oneThroughFour)
// => (2 3 4)

carOf(cdrOf(oneThroughFour))
// => 2

cons(VInt(10), oneThroughFour)
// => (10 1 2 3 4)

cons(VInt(5), oneThroughFour)
// => (5 1 2 3 4)
@end example

@noindent
The value of @code{VNil}, used to terminate the chain of pairs, can be thought
of as a sequence of no elements, the @newterm{empty list}.  The word
@newterm{nil} is a contraction of the Latin word @emph{nihil}, which means
``nothing.''@footnote{It's remarkable how much energy in the standardization of
Lisp dialects has been dissipated in arguments that are literally over nothing:
Should @code{nil} be an ordinary name?  Should the value of @code{nil} be a
symbol?  Should it be a list?  Should it be a pair?  In this edition the
end-of-list marker is the runtime's own object, @code{VNil}, so the question
never arises: it is a value, it is not a name, and it prints as @code{()}.
Once we have introduced quotation in @ref{2.3}, we will denote the empty list
as @code{'()} and dispense with the variable entirely.}

@subsubheading List operations

The use of pairs to represent sequences of elements as lists is accompanied by
conventional programming techniques for manipulating lists by successively
``cdring down'' the lists---walking rest by rest toward the end-of-list
marker.  For example, the procedure @code{listRef}
takes as arguments a list and a number @math{n} and returns the @math{n^{\hbox{th}}} item of
the list.  It is customary to number the elements of the list beginning with 0.
The method for computing @code{listRef} is the following:

@itemize @bullet

@item
For @math{{n = 0}}, @code{listRef} should return the head of the list.

@item
Otherwise, @code{listRef} should return  the @math{{(n - 1)}}-st item of the
rest of the list.

@end itemize

@example kotlin
fun listRef(
    items: Value,
    n: Long,
): Value = if (n == 0L) carOf(items) else listRef(cdrOf(items), n - 1L)

val squares = vlist(VInt(1), VInt(4), VInt(9), VInt(16), VInt(25))

listRef(squares, 3)
// => 16
@end example

@noindent
Often we cdr down the whole list.  To aid in this, the chain representation
includes the end-of-list marker itself, and testing against it---the role
Scheme's @code{null?} plays---tells us whether the argument is the empty list.
The procedure @code{lengthList}, which returns the number of items in a
list, illustrates this typical pattern of use:

@example kotlin
fun lengthList(items: Value): Long = if (items is VNil) 0L else 1L + lengthList(cdrOf(items))

val odds = vlist(VInt(1), VInt(3), VInt(5), VInt(7))

lengthList(odds)
// => 4
@end example

@noindent
The @code{lengthList} procedure implements a simple recursive plan. The reduction
step is:

@itemize @bullet

@item
The @code{lengthList} of any list is 1 plus the @code{lengthList} of the rest of
the list.

@end itemize

@noindent
This is applied successively until we reach the base case:

@itemize @bullet

@item
The @code{lengthList} of the empty list is 0.

@end itemize

@noindent
We could also compute @code{lengthList} in an iterative style, which Kotlin
makes explicit with @code{tailrec}:

@example kotlin
fun lengthListIter(items: Value): Long @{
    tailrec fun iter(
        a: Value,
        count: Long,
    ): Long = if (a is VNil) count else iter(cdrOf(a), count + 1L)

    return iter(items, 0L)
@}
@end example

@noindent
Another conventional programming technique is to ``cons up'' an answer
list while cdring down a list, as in the procedure @code{appendList}, which
takes two lists as arguments and combines their elements to make a new list:

@example kotlin
appendList(squares, odds)
// => (1 4 9 16 25 1 3 5 7)

appendList(odds, squares)
// => (1 3 5 7 1 4 9 16 25)
@end example

@noindent
@code{AppendList} is also implemented using a recursive plan.  To append
lists @code{list1} and @code{list2}, do the following:

@itemize @bullet

@item
If @code{list1} is the empty list, then the result is just @code{list2}.

@item
Otherwise, append the rest of @code{list1} and @code{list2}, and
cons the head of @code{list1} onto the result:

@end itemize

@example kotlin
fun appendList(
    list1: Value,
    list2: Value,
): Value = if (list1 is VNil) list2 else cons(carOf(list1), appendList(cdrOf(list1), list2))
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.17}Exercise 2.17:} Define a procedure
@code{lastPair} that returns the list that contains only the last element of a
given (nonempty) list:

@example kotlin
lastPair(vlist(VInt(23), VInt(72), VInt(149), VInt(34)))
// => (34)
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.18}Exercise 2.18:} Define a procedure @code{reverseList}
that takes a list as argument and returns a list of the same elements in
reverse order:

@example kotlin
reverseList(vlist(VInt(1), VInt(4), VInt(9), VInt(16), VInt(25)))
// => (25 16 9 4 1)
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.19}Exercise 2.19:} Consider the change-counting
program of @ref{1.2.2}.  It would be nice to be able to easily change
the currency used by the program, so that we could compute the number of ways
to change a British pound, for example.  As the program is written, the
knowledge of the currency is distributed partly into the procedure
@code{firstDenomination} and partly into the procedure @code{cc}
(which knows that there are five kinds of U.S. coins).  It would be nicer to be
able to supply a list of coins to be used for making change.

We want to rewrite the procedure @code{cc} so that its second argument is a
list of the values of the coins to use rather than an integer specifying which
coins to use.  We could then have lists that defined each kind of currency:

@example kotlin
val usCoins = listOf(50L, 25L, 10L, 5L, 1L)

// the half-penny of the book's pound is not a whole number of cents,
// so this edition's pound carries seven denominations, not eight
val ukCoins = listOf(100L, 50L, 20L, 10L, 5L, 2L, 1L)
@end example

We could then call @code{cc} as follows:

@example kotlin
cc(100L, usCoins)
// => 292
@end example

To do this will require changing the program @code{cc} somewhat.  It will still
have the same form, but it will access its second argument differently, as
follows:

@example kotlin
fun cc(
    amount: Long,
    coinValues: List<Long>,
): Long =
    when @{
        amount == 0L -> 1L
        amount < 0L || noMore(coinValues) -> 0L
        else ->
            cc(amount, exceptFirstDenomination(coinValues)) +
                cc(amount - firstDenomination(coinValues), coinValues)
    @}
@end example

Define the procedures @code{firstDenomination},
@code{exceptFirstDenomination} and @code{noMore} in terms of primitive
operations on list structures.  Does the order of the list @code{coinValues}
affect the answer produced by @code{cc}?  Why or why not?
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.20}Exercise 2.20:} The procedures @code{+},
@code{*}, and @code{listOf} take arbitrary numbers of arguments. One way to
define such procedures is to declare the last parameter @code{vararg}, Kotlin's
replacement for Scheme's @newterm{dotted-tail notation}.
In a function declaration, a parameter marked @code{vararg} indicates that,
when the function is called, the initial parameters (if any) will receive the
initial arguments, as usual, while the @code{vararg} parameter will hold all
remaining arguments as an array.  For instance, given the definition

@example kotlin
fun f(x: Long, y: Long, vararg z: Long) @{
    ⟨body⟩
@}
@end example

@noindent
the function @code{f} can be called with two or more arguments.  If we evaluate

@example kotlin
f(1L, 2L, 3L, 4L, 5L, 6L)
@end example

@noindent
then in the body of @code{f}, @code{x} will be 1, @code{y} will be 2, and
@code{z} will hold the values @code{3, 4, 5, 6}.  Given the definition

@example kotlin
fun g(vararg w: Long) @{
    ⟨body⟩
@}
@end example

@noindent
the function @code{g} can be called with zero or more arguments.  If we
evaluate

@example kotlin
g(1L, 2L, 3L, 4L, 5L, 6L)
@end example

@noindent
then in the body of @code{g}, @code{w} will hold the values @code{1, 2, 3, 4, 5,
6}.@footnote{Kotlin's function types cannot spell a vararg parameter, so the
lambda form of @code{f} in the book's footnote has no direct spelling: a
vararg function must be declared as a named @code{fun}, which can still be
passed around by reference as @code{::f}.}

Use this notation to write a procedure @code{sameParity} that takes one or
more integers and returns a list of all the arguments that have the same
even-odd parity as the first argument.  For example,

@example kotlin
sameParity(1L, 2L, 3L, 4L, 5L, 6L, 7L)
// => [1, 3, 5, 7]

sameParity(2L, 3L, 4L, 5L, 6L, 7L)
// => [2, 4, 6]
@end example
@end quotation

@subsubheading Mapping over lists

One extremely useful operation is to apply some transformation to each element
in a list and generate the list of results.  For instance, the following
procedure scales each number in a list by a given factor:

@example kotlin
fun scaleList(
    items: Value,
    factor: Long,
): Value =
    if (items is VNil) @{
        VNil
    @} else @{
        cons(VInt(numOf(carOf(items)) * factor), scaleList(cdrOf(items), factor))
    @}

scaleList(vlist(VInt(1), VInt(2), VInt(3), VInt(4), VInt(5)), 10)
// => (10 20 30 40 50)
@end example

@noindent
We can abstract this general idea and capture it as a common pattern expressed
as a higher-order procedure, just as in @ref{1.3}.  The higher-order
procedure here is called @code{mapList}.  @code{MapList} takes as arguments a
function of one argument and a list, and returns a list of the results produced by
applying the function to each element in the list:@footnote{@anchor{Footnote 78}
Kotlin's own @code{map} on lists is the everyday twin of the one described
here.  The standard library's @code{zip} plays the role of Scheme's
@math{n}-ary @code{map}, which takes a function of @math{n} arguments, together
with @math{n} lists, and applies the function to all the first elements of the
lists, all the second elements of the lists, and so on, returning a list of the
results.  For example:

@example kotlin
listOf(1L, 2L, 3L).zip(listOf(40L, 50L, 60L)).zip(listOf(700L, 800L, 900L)) @{ ab, c -> ab.first + ab.second + c @}
// => [741, 852, 963]

listOf(1L, 2L, 3L).zip(listOf(4L, 5L, 6L)) @{ x, y -> x + 2L * y @}
// => [9, 12, 15]
@end example
}

@example kotlin
fun mapList(
    f: (Long) -> Long,
    items: Value,
): Value = if (items is VNil) VNil else cons(VInt(f(numOf(carOf(items)))), mapList(f, cdrOf(items)))
@end example

@noindent
For example:

@example kotlin
mapList(@{ x -> kotlin.math.abs(x) @}, vlist(VInt(-10), VInt(2), VInt(-11), VInt(17)))
// => (10 2 11 17)

mapList(@{ x -> x * x @}, vlist(VInt(1), VInt(2), VInt(3), VInt(4)))
// => (1 4 9 16)
@end example

@noindent
Now we can give a new definition of @code{scaleList} in terms of @code{mapList}.
Kotlin cannot rebind the top-level name @code{scaleList} the way a second
Scheme @code{define} would, so this edition names the new definition
@code{scaleListViaMap}:

@example kotlin
fun scaleListViaMap(
    items: Value,
    factor: Long,
): Value = mapList(@{ x -> x * factor @}, items)
@end example

@noindent
@code{MapList} is an important construct, not only because it captures a common
pattern, but because it establishes a higher level of abstraction in dealing
with lists.  In the original definition of @code{scaleList}, the recursive
structure of the program draws attention to the element-by-element processing
of the list.  Defining @code{scaleList} in terms of @code{mapList} suppresses that
level of detail and emphasizes that scaling transforms a list of elements to a
list of results.  The difference between the two definitions is not that the
computer is performing a different process (it isn't) but that we think about
the process differently.  In effect, @code{mapList} helps establish an abstraction
barrier that isolates the implementation of procedures that transform lists
from the details of how the elements of the list are extracted and combined.
Like the barriers shown in @ref{Figure 2.1}, this abstraction gives us the
flexibility to change the low-level details of how sequences are implemented,
while preserving the conceptual framework of operations that transform
sequences to sequences.  Section @ref{2.2.3} expands on this use of sequences
as a framework for organizing programs.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.21}Exercise 2.21:} The procedure @code{squareList}
takes a list of numbers as argument and returns a list of the squares of those
numbers.

@example kotlin
squareList(vlist(VInt(1), VInt(2), VInt(3), VInt(4)))
// => (1 4 9 16)
@end example

Here are two different definitions of @code{squareList}.  Complete both of
them by filling in the missing expressions:

@example kotlin
fun squareList(items: Value): Value =
    if (items is VNil) VNil else cons(⟨??⟩, ⟨??⟩)

fun squareList(items: Value): Value = mapList(⟨??⟩, ⟨??⟩)
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.22}Exercise 2.22:} Louis Reasoner tries to rewrite
the first @code{squareList} procedure of @ref{Exercise 2.21} so that it
evolves an iterative process:

@example kotlin
fun squareList(items: Value): Value @{
    fun iter(
        things: Value,
        answer: Value,
    ): Value =
        if (things is VNil) @{
            answer
        @} else @{
            iter(cdrOf(things), cons(VInt(square(numOf(carOf(things)))), answer))
        @}

    return iter(items, VNil)
@}
@end example

Unfortunately, defining @code{squareList} this way produces the answer list in
the reverse order of the one desired.  Why?

Louis then tries to fix his bug by interchanging the arguments to @code{cons}:

@example kotlin
fun squareList(items: Value): Value @{
    fun iter(
        things: Value,
        answer: Value,
    ): Value =
        if (things is VNil) @{
            answer
        @} else @{
            iter(cdrOf(things), cons(answer, VInt(square(numOf(carOf(things))))))
        @}

    return iter(items, VNil)
@}
@end example

