@caption{@strong{Figure 2.6:} The list structure in @ref{Figure 2.5} viewed as a tree.}
@end float

@noindent
For a Kotlin compiler, a tree whose leaves are numbers is a recursive data
type: either a @newterm{leaf} holding one number, or a node holding the list
of its subtrees.  This edition declares it as a sealed hierarchy, which makes
the case split explicit and lets the compiler check every traversal:

@example kotlin
sealed interface Tree @{
    data class Leaf(val value: Long) : Tree

    data class Node(val subtrees: List<Tree>) : Tree
@}

fun leaf(value: Long): Tree = Tree.Leaf(value)

fun tree(vararg subtrees: Tree): Tree = Tree.Node(subtrees.toList())
@end example

@noindent
Recursion is a natural tool for dealing with tree structures, since we can
often reduce operations on trees to operations on their branches, which reduce
in turn to operations on the branches of the branches, and so on, until we
reach the leaves of the tree.  As an example, compare the @code{lengthList}
procedure of @ref{2.2.1} with the @code{countLeaves} procedure, which
returns the total number of leaves of a tree.  The chain
@code{((1 2) 3 4)} has length 3:

@example kotlin
val x = cons(vlist(VInt(1), VInt(2)), vlist(VInt(3), VInt(4)))

lengthList(x)
// => 3
@end example

@noindent
while the same structure as a tree has four leaves:

@example kotlin
val xAsTree = tree(tree(leaf(1), leaf(2)), leaf(3), leaf(4))

countLeaves(xAsTree)
// => 4

val doubleX = tree(xAsTree, xAsTree)
// the book's (list x x): two branches, eight leaves

doubleX.subtrees.size
// => 2

countLeaves(doubleX)
// => 8
@end example

@noindent
To implement @code{countLeaves}, recall the recursive plan for computing
@code{lengthList}:

@itemize @bullet

@item
The @code{lengthList} of a list @code{x} is 1 plus @code{lengthList} of the
rest of @code{x}.

@item
@code{lengthList} of the empty list is 0.

@end itemize

@noindent
@code{countLeaves} is similar.  The value for the empty list is the same:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{countLeaves} of the empty tree is 0.

@end itemize

@noindent
But in the reduction step, where we move from a node to its subtrees, we
must take into account that a subtree may itself be a tree whose leaves we
need to count.  Thus, the appropriate reduction step is

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{countLeaves} of a tree @code{x} is the sum of @code{countLeaves} over
the subtrees of @code{x}.

@end itemize

@noindent
Finally, by following subtrees we reach actual leaves, so we need another base
case:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{countLeaves} of a leaf is 1.

@end itemize

@noindent
To aid in writing recursive procedures on trees, Scheme provides the primitive
predicate @code{pair?}, which tests whether its argument is a pair.  In this
edition the check is a type test, and the compiler enforces it: a @code{when}
over the sealed @code{Tree} must cover both the leaf and the node case, and it
cannot miss either.  Here is
the complete procedure:@footnote{The order of the first two clauses in the
book's @code{cond} matters, since the empty list satisfies @code{null?} and also is
not a pair.  The sealed hierarchy dissolves that ordering question: a
@code{Leaf} and a @code{Node} are different types, so no case can shadow
another.}

@example kotlin
fun countLeaves(t: Tree): Long =
    when (t) @{
        is Tree.Leaf -> 1L
        is Tree.Node -> t.subtrees.sumOf(::countLeaves)
    @}
@end example

